Original commit body from 9aa592caee6e2cd5eb23e34b14bbeb241923cc69:

Addresses INV-bamij, third batch (after #1234 ruby/lua and #1235
rust/swift/kotlin/scala). Java field initialisers, static {} blocks and
field-bound lambdas, and C/C++ global initialisers and file-scope macro
invocations, emitted no calls edge.

- java: method, else the enclosing class/interface/enum/record (via the
  analyzer's own (path, line, col) position index), else the file.
- c: function, else the file, but only when the call is inside NO
  function_definition.
- cpp: the walk starts from the file anchor, and entering a
  function_definition resets the context, so an unnamed function does not
  inherit the file. The stack-object-construction arm stays function-only.

THE FIRST CUT OVER-REACHED, and the read-back caught it. On sherpa-onnx cpp,
81 of 166 new edges were calls inside functions the analyzer could not name
(C: 12 of 18 on crun). Attributing those to the file would hide a
symbol-lookup gap behind a plausible edge, so they stay unemitted. At file
scope the construction arm read `extern Widget w;` and member prototypes as
constructions. And GNU `__attribute__ ((format (...)))` / `#if
__has_attribute(x)` parse as calls; c_family_is_not_a_call() excludes them.
Each narrowing is pinned by a test that goes red without it.

